   "source": [
    "# Now overlay the REAL in-degree distribution (binned) on the same log-log axes as the two specimens.\n",
    "xp, yp = hist_density(power_samples, log_bins)\n",
    "xe, ye = hist_density(exp_samples, log_bins)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 5.5))\n",
    "ax.plot(xp, yp, \"o\", ms=4, color=\"tab:purple\", alpha=0.6, label=\"power law (synthetic)\")\n",
    "ax.plot(xe, ye, \"o\", ms=4, color=\"tab:green\", alpha=0.6, label=\"exponential (synthetic)\")\n",
    "ax.plot(u_in_binned, P_in_binned, \"o-\", ms=6, color=\"darkred\", label=\"real Marvel in-degree (binned)\")\n",
    "ax.set_xscale(\"log\"); ax.set_yscale(\"log\")\n",
    "ax.set_xlabel(\"x  (in-degree + 1, log)\"); ax.set_ylabel(\"density (log)\")\n",
    "ax.set_title(\"Real in-degree vs. two mathematical specimens\")\n",
    "ax.legend()\n",
    "plt.tight_layout()\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3e4eefe6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Which does it resemble?** Over most of its range the real in-degree curve tracks the power-law specimen's straight-line slope on log–log axes far more closely than the exponential's, which visibly bends over and drops off long before Spider-Man's neighborhood. That said — with only $n=303$ nodes and a single realization of the network, this eyeball comparison isn't a statistical test; a lognormal could plausibly produce a similarly straightish binned tail over this short a range too. The course page's own closing note applies here: the real way to settle \"is it a power law?\" rigorously is the cumulative distribution function, no binning required — that's week 2's tool, not this week's.\n",
    "\n",
    "*(Your own read of \"how sure are you\" belongs here — write a sentence or two once you've looked at the plot yourself.)*"
   ]
  },
